From b31f97d4cb7abf37683c815afc099b3ae173bc61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 03:36:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] filesystem: a refused address is asked again where it came
 from, and a server that allows one connection gets one

A streaming add-on hands Kodi the address of its resolver, which redirects to the
service's CDN with a token in the address. The token runs out after a while,
and a CDN may allow one or two connections to a file. Kodi kept only the
address it was sent to, and opened a second connection on every seek (the
multiple sessions of CCurlFile::Seek()). Measured in a VM with a server that
behaves like such a CDN (an image of this tree before the change, the
add-on's address redirecting to the CDN):
- a CDN that allows one connection: the seek's second connection was refused
  (HTTP 403) and the video never went on (two seeks, 61 s each, then the
  player gave the source up);
- with two, from a server that sends at 450 KiB/s, the third seek was refused
  the same way while it still counted the connection just closed;
- a token that ran out while the video played: the next seek was refused and
  playback ended with an error.

Now:
- XFILE::CHttpSourceMemory keeps, for all of Kodi, where an address
  redirected to (the latest 64), the servers that allow one connection to a
  file, and the addresses that only the type their server gave says are disc
  images. An address is kept without the options Kodi adds after '|', and is
  never logged whole (ShortForLog(): the host and the name of the file,
  nothing between that may be a token).
- CCurlFile::Seek(), when the new connection is refused with 403, 429 or 503
  while another connection of the file is open, closes that one and asks
  again at once, and once more after 0.3 s (a server notices a closed
  connection only when it next writes to it); the file is read over one
  connection from then on. When one connection took what two did not, at the
  same address, every file of that server is read over one.
- A refusal of the address itself (401, 403, 404, 410) is then asked again,
  once, at the address it came from through a redirect: its own, or the one
  the memory knows (the player opens the address its stream factory
  resolved), which sends to a new one; CCurlFile::Open() does the same for an
  address refused at once (a token used by an earlier request).
- Still refused by a server that allows one connection: asked again after
  0.5 s and after 1 s more.
- The subtitle search after a seek (CSubtitleSeekSearch) reads the file over
  a connection of its own: not from a server known to allow one.
Each step says what it did in one INFO line. Where no server refuses
anything, nothing changes: the second session of a seek is kept as before.
What happens when the address it came from is refused too is as before
(CFileCache gives the source up and the player ends with an error).

Measured after, in the same VM: one connection allowed, the first seek's
second connection was refused once and the file then read over one, and the
seeks went on in 1.5 s, as with two; the token run out, the seek was refused
three times (at once, at once over one connection, after 0.3 s), the address
was asked again where it came from, and the video went on in 1.8 s; at
450 KiB/s with two, every seek went on in 1.8 s.
